==English==

===Etymology===
Attested since the 16th century; from {{uder|en|frm|-}} ''<span lang="frm">demoliss-</span>'', the stem of some conjugated forms of the verb {{m|frm|demolir||to destroy”, “to tear down}}, from {{uder|en|la|dēmōlior||I tear down}}. Displaced native {{ncog|ang|tōweorpan|lit=to throw apart}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/dəˈmɒl.ɪʃ/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-demolish.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}} {{tlb|en|transitive}}

# {{lb|en|literally}} To [[destroy]] (of buildings, especially in a planned and intentional fashion).
#: {{ux|en|They '''demolished''' the old mill and put up four townhouses.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1961|month=January|title=Talking of Trains: The Severn Bridge disaster|journal=Trains Illustrated|page=5|text=The force of the explosion '''demolished''' one pair of piers and two spans of the bridge crashed down into the river on top of the barges.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2020 May 6|author=Graeme Pickering|title=Borders Railway: time for the next step|journal=Rail|page=53|text=At [[Hawick]], nothing remains of the station, and the viaduct over the River [[Teviot]] is one of a number of bridges that were '''demolished''' or removed following closure.}}
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} To [[defeat]] or [[consume]] utterly (as a [[theory]], [[belief]] or [[opponent]]).
#* '''1992''', Robert Rankin, ''The Antipope'' (page 68)
#*: The Captain folded his brow into a look of intense perplexity. 'You seem exceedingly spry for a man who '''demolished''' an entire bottle of brandy and better part of an ounce of shag in a single evening.'<br>'And very nice too,' said the tramp. 'Now as to breakfast?'
#* {{quote-journal|en
|date=October 2, 2011
|author=Kevin Core
|title=Fulham 6 - 0 QPR
|work=BBC Sport
|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/eng_prem/15051267.stm
|page=
|passage=Andrew Johnson scored a hat-trick as Fulham '''demolished''' London rivals Queens Park Rangers to win their Premier League fixture of the season.}}

